'''Montgomery Blair''' (May 10, 1813 – July 27, 1883) was an American politician and lawyer from [[Maryland]]. He served in the [[Lincoln administration]] [[United States Cabinet|cabinet]] as [[United States Postmaster General|Postmaster-General]] from 1861 to 1864, during the [[American Civil War|Civil War]]. He was the son of [[Francis Preston Blair]], elder brother of [[Francis Preston Blair Jr.]] and cousin of [[B. Gratz Brown]].

==Early life and education==
Blair was born in [[Franklin County, Kentucky]], site of the state capital of [[Frankfort, Kentucky|Frankfort]].<ref name= prominent/> His father, [[Francis Preston Blair|Francis Preston Blair, Sr.]],<ref name= prominent/> was editor of ''The Washington Globe'' and a prominent figure in the [[Democratic Party (US)|Democratic Party]] during the [[Jacksonian democracy|Jacksonian era]]. As a boy, Montgomery "often listened to the talk of his father and [[Andrew Jackson]]."<ref>Allan Nevins, ''The War for the Union'', vol. 1: ''The Improvised War, 1861-1862'' (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1959), p. 43.</ref>

Blair graduated from the [[United States Military Academy]] at [[West Point, New York|West Point]]. After a year of service in the [[Seminole War]], however, he left the [[United States Army|U.S. Army]], married Caroline Rebecca Buckner of [[Virginia]], and began studying law at [[Transylvania University]] in [[Lexington, Kentucky]].

==Career==

Blair began to practice law in 1839 in [[St. Louis]], Missouri.  He served as [[United States district attorney|U.S. District Attorney]] from 1839 to 1843 and later as judge of the court of common pleas from 1843 to 1849. After the death of his first wife, Carolina, in 1844, he later married Mary Elizabeth Woodbury, daughter of [[Levi Woodbury]].<ref>Moroney, p. 5;https://hdl.handle.net/2027/mdp.39015016417332 {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240208220354/https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015016417332 |date=February 8, 2024 }}</ref><ref name= prominent/>

Blair moved to [[Maryland]] in 1852 and devoted himself to the practice of law, principally in the [[Supreme Court of the United States]].<ref name= prominent/> He was United States Solicitor in the Court of Claims from 1855 to 1858.<ref name= prominent/>

